    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Post-frostbite neuropathic pain of the fingers, partly controlled (M79.2)</content>. Average pain down from 6 to 5 of 10 on capsaicin cream; not enough. Of the first-line oral drugs in NICE CG173 (amitriptyline, duloxetine, gabapentin, pregabalin), duloxetine is chosen because it is the least likely to cause drowsiness, which matters for driving and flying, and because it has no anticholinergic effects. Blood pressure 126/78 before starting. [S]<list><item>Duloxetine 30 mg daily for a week, then 60 mg daily; take with food.</item><item>Continue capsaicin cream if it helps.</item><item>Do not take tramadol or other serotonergic medicines; ask before any new medicine.</item><item>Do not stop duloxetine suddenly.</item><item>Sodium and ALT in two to four weeks.</item><item>Review in about six weeks; the preventive visit in two weeks will also check how he is tolerating it.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Pain severity (0 to 10 numeric rating) and overall improvement</td><td>Every review, and early after starting or changing a medicine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Average 5 of 10, from 6</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Adverse effects, especially drowsiness, dizziness and nausea</td><td>Every review, and early after starting or changing a medicine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Stinging from capsaicin in the first week only; duloxetine started today</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Daily activities and participation: fine hand tasks in the cold, driving, flying</td><td>Every review, and early after starting or changing a medicine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Still fumbling outdoors; driving safely</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Mood (PHQ-9) and anxiety</td><td>Every review, and early after starting or changing a medicine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Mood fair by interview; PHQ-9 due at the next review</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Sleep quality</td><td>Every review, and early after starting or changing a medicine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Woken some nights</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Blood pressure</td><td>Before starting duloxetine and periodically on it</td><td>Completed today</td><td>126/78 mmHg before starting duloxetine</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Sodium and liver enzyme (ALT) on duloxetine</td><td>Baseline, then once in the first weeks of treatment</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Baseline at intake (sodium 141 mmol/L, ALT 24 U/L); repeat ordered for two to four weeks after starting</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Interactions: no tramadol or other serotonergic drugs; caution with oral NSAIDs; alcohol</td><td>When starting, then every review</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No tramadol or other serotonergic drugs; rare alcohol</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Skin of the fingers and cold protection</td><td>Every review, and early after starting or changing a medicine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Skin intact; cold protection continued</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Average finger pain at or below 3 of 10, with fewer cold attacks, by four months. In progress; target 1928-06-09 [S]</item><item>Able to handle buttons, keys and a pen outdoors in the cold with protective gloves. In progress; target 1928-05-05 [S]</item><item>Any medicine used causes no daytime drowsiness that affects driving or flying. In progress; target 1928-06-09 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Neuropathic pain in adults: pharmacological management in non-specialist settings (CG173) (2013, updated 2020); Wilderness Medical Society Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Prevention and Treatment of Frostbite: 2024 Update (McIntosh SE, et al. Wilderness Environ Med 2024;35(2):183-197) (2024)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Preventive visit in two weeks. Neuropathic pain review in six weeks. Next scheduled visit 1928-04-07: periodic adult preventive visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
